hostname r2
!
ip routing
!
! -------------------------------------------------------------------
! r2 — ABR between Area 2 and Area 1 (transit area for virtual link)
!
! r2 has interfaces in:
!   - Area 2: the "disconnected" area that needs backbone access
!   - Area 1: the transit area used by the virtual link
!
! WITHOUT virtual link:
!   r2 is an ABR between area 2 and area 1, but area 1 is itself
!   not connected to area 0 through r2. Area 2 routes cannot reach
!   area 0 routers like r4.
!
! WITH virtual link:
!   r2 and r3 form a virtual link through area 1.
!   The virtual link extends area 0 logically through area 1.
!   r2 becomes an ABR between area 2 AND area 0 (via the virtual link).
!   Area 2 can now exchange routes with area 0.
!
! The virtual link configuration on r2:
!   area 1 virtual-link 10.0.0.3
!   (10.0.0.3 is r3's router-id — the other end of the virtual link)
!
! Ethernet1: connects to r1 (10.1.12.0/30, r2=.2) — Area 2
! Ethernet2: connects to r3 (10.1.23.0/30, r2=.1) — Area 1 (transit)
! Loopback0:   loopback 10.0.0.2/32 — Area 1 (or Area 2; see note below)
! -------------------------------------------------------------------

interface Loopback0
 ip address 10.0.0.2/32

interface Ethernet1
 no switchport
 description to-r1 (OSPF area 2)
 ip address 10.1.12.2/30

interface Ethernet2
 no switchport
 description to-r3 (OSPF area 1, transit for virtual link)
 ip address 10.1.23.1/30

! -------------------------------------------------------------------
! TASK: Configure OSPF on r2 (ABR: area 2 and area 1)
!
! Step 1 — Basic OSPF without virtual link:
!
!   router ospf 1
!    router-id 10.0.0.2
!    network 10.0.0.2/32 area 1
!    network 10.1.12.0/30 area 2
!    network 10.1.23.0/30 area 1
!
!   Note: loopback is placed in area 1 so it is reachable from r3
!   for the virtual link peer address lookup.
!
!   Verify (before virtual link):
!     show ip ospf neighbor     <- r1 (area 2) and r3 (area 1)
!     show ip route ospf       <- area 2 and area 1 routes, but NOT area 0
!
! Step 2 — Add virtual link to r3 (through area 1):
!
!   router ospf 1
!    area 1 virtual-link 10.0.0.3
!
!   This creates a logical link between r2 and r3.
!   The virtual link runs OSPF over the area 1 path as if r2 and r3
!   share an area 0 link.
!
!   Verify:
!     show ip ospf virtual-links
!     -> should show "Virtual Link OSPF_VL0 to router 10.0.0.3"
!     -> state should be "Point-to-Point, OI"
!
!   After virtual link is up:
!     show ip ospf neighbor
!     -> r3 appears TWICE: as area 1 neighbor AND as virtual-link neighbor
!
!   From r1 (after virtual link):
!     ping 10.0.0.4 source 10.0.0.1  <- should now work
! -------------------------------------------------------------------
